<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Nepomuceno Almonte</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1803">1803</a>–<a href="/years/1869">1869</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Nepomuceno Almonte was a Mexican soldier and diplomat, son of the independence leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos</a>. A conservative, he supported French intervention and headed the regency that governed in the name of the Second Mexican Empire from 1863 until Maximilian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> arrived in 1864. He then served as the emperor's grand marshal and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-nepomuceno-almonte.jpg" alt="Juan Nepomuceno Almonte" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cruces y Campa, México, 2d de S. Francisco, no. 4 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Nepomuceno_Almonte_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Almonte was born on 15 May 1803 at Nocupétaro in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a>, the son of the priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos</a> and Brígida Almonte, who was said to be of Amerindian ancestry. In 1815 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morelos">Morelos</a> sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, where he was educated, learned fluent English and worked as a clerk; his stay ended when his father was executed on 22 December 1815. Between 1822 and 1824 he served on the staff of José Félix Trespalacios in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and then joined the Mexican delegation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, helping José Mariano Michelena negotiate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s first treaty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In early life a partisan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-guerrero">Vicente Guerrero</a> and an editor of the progressive newspaper Atleta, Almonte went into hiding when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-bustamante">Anastasio Bustamante</a> came to power. In 1836 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> as aide-de-camp to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a>, kept a journal at the Alamo, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-san-jacinto">Battle of San Jacinto</a> on 21 April 1836 surrendered to Thomas Jefferson Rusk after leading the last organised resistance. He shared Santa Anna's captivity as interpreter and went with him to meet President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a> in Washington.</p><p>As minister of war under the centralist republic he led loyal troops to recover the National Palace during the Federalist Revolt of 1840, and he denounced Jose Maria Gutierrez Estrada's essay calling for a European prince as scandalous and treasonous. Sent as minister to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1841, he lobbied against intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and asked for his passport when the Senate voted to annex it. His biographer Rivera Cambas wrote that this convinced him that American expansion must be opposed even at the cost of European intervention. He was minister of war again during the war with the United States and ran for president in 1845, 1848 and 1852.</p><p>As minister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Almonte began to lobby for intervention, signed the Mon-Almonte Treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on 27 September 1859 and, on 31 October 1861, with other monarchists signed the London Convention. After the French took the capital in 1863 he was one of the three-man executive chosen by the Junta Superior, which became the regency of the empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Almonte was serving as a diplomat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> when the French withdrew their troops, and he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 21 March 1869, two years after the empire fell. The town of Almonte in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> was named after him in 1855, as was Almont in Michigan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan Almonte” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Juan Nepomuceno Almonte</b> headed a regency until Maximilian and his wife Carlota of Belgium arrived in May 1864.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-mexican-empire">Second Mexican Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.21,-101.91&amp;year=1845" aria-label="Michoacán on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1795 1452 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juan Nepomuceno Almonte"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2245 1722h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2245 1722h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.21,-101.91&amp;year=1845">What was here in 1845? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
